meta {
  name: Transfer - Retrieve
  type: http
  seq: 8
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/accession/transfers/{{transferId}}/retrieve
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{businessAccessToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "notes": "Reverting one step"
  }
}

tests {
  test("201 Created", function () { expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201); });
}

docs {
  Retrieve (§B.13) — the receiving-side UNDO. Moves the transfer BACK exactly one
  status step along the receiving lifecycle, so it can be clicked repeatedly to walk
  the status back to the start:

    PICKED_UP -> IN_TRANSIT
    RECEIVED  -> PICKED_UP
    ACCEPTED  -> RECEIVED
    REPEAT    -> RECEIVED
    REJECTED  -> RECEIVED

  From IN_TRANSIT there is nothing for the receiver to undo, so Retrieve is refused
  with 422 INVALID_TRANSFER_TRANSITION (and is not offered in the UI at that status).

  This is a MINIMAL step-back: it changes only `transferStatus` (and clears the
  just-undone step's own field — pickedUpAt/pickedUpBy, receivedAt/receiveCondition,
  or acceptedAt) and writes a `retrieve` row on the sample's history timeline.

  ACCEPTED -> RECEIVED is the one step that also touches the receiving copy: Accept
  materialises a clone in the receiving branch's In-House list (RULE 1), so the undo
  soft-deletes that clone and moves its report(s) back to origin
  (`removeTransferCloneInTx`), clearing `clonedSampleId` — otherwise a later re-Accept
  would create a DUPLICATE in-house sample. It is REFUSED with 422
  TRANSFER_RETRIEVE_BLOCKED when the receiver has already started working the copy (its
  status moved past ACCEPTED — collected / resulted / passed on); Reject or Repeat it
  instead. It does NOT reverse a sender-side return (REPEAT/REJECTED -> RECEIVED simply
  reopens the transfer). It is distinct from the SENDER's sample-level Retrieve
  (`POST order-samples/:id/retrieve`), which recalls a sent sample to origin.

  The update is conditional on the transfer's current status, so a concurrent change
  fails with 422 INVALID_TRANSFER_TRANSITION instead of overwriting it.

  Who may act (INTERNAL transfer): only the RECEIVING branch — the caller's active
  branch (the profile they switched to) must be the transfer's destination, otherwise
  403 TRANSFER_WRONG_BRANCH and nothing changes. A transfer whose test was removed from
  the order is frozen: 409 TRANSFER_ITEM_REMOVED.

  Body: `notes` is optional (max 1000). No required field.

  Group action: `POST accession/transfers/bulk/retrieve` with `{ ids: [...],
  skipInvalid?: true }`. With `skipInvalid: true`, a transfer with nothing to undo
  (IN_TRANSIT) or a frozen one is SKIPPED and listed in `skipped` with its reason; the
  rest still move. Without it, the first refusal is raised as an error.

  Permission: assertAny of the per-kind retrieve keys
  (accession:{internal_referral_order,external_referral_order,outsource_orders}__retrieve_the_sample).
}
